<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">National Museum of Indonesia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1778">1778</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Museum Gajah, Museum Nasional Indonesia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The National Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, popularly called Museum Gajah, is the main archaeological and historical museum of Indonesia, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a>. It grew from the collections of the Batavian Society of Arts and Sciences, founded in 1778, and holds stone inscriptions, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> statues, ceramics and ethnographic objects, among them the Portuguese padrão of 1522 from the Sunda Kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/national-museum-of-indonesia.jpg" alt="National Museum of Indonesia" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chainwit. · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Front_facade_-_National_Museum_Indonesia,_Jakarta_(2025)_-_img_07.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>On 24 April 1778 a group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> intellectuals founded the Bataviaasch Genootschap van Kunsten en Wetenschappen, the Batavian Society of Arts and Sciences. This private body aimed to promote research in history, archaeology, ethnography and physics. One founder, J. C. M. Radermacher, gave a building in Old Batavia together with a collection of objects and books, which started the society's museum and library.</p><p>In 1862 the colonial government decided to build a museum to house and display the collections, and it opened in 1868. It is popularly called Gedung Gajah, the Elephant Building, after a bronze elephant given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chulalongkorn">Chulalongkorn</a> of Siam in 1871. In 1931 a fire at the World Colonial Exposition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> destroyed most of the objects sent there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After independence the institution was renamed the Indonesian Cultural Institute in February 1950. It was handed to the Indonesian government on 17 September 1962 as the Museum Pusat, the Central Museum, and became the Museum Nasional in 1979. A 1977 agreement between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> returned some treasures, among them the treasures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombok">Lombok</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagarakretagama">Nagarakretagama</a> lontar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prajnaparamita">Prajnaparamita</a> statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>.</p><p>A new wing opened in 2007. Four gold objects of the 10th-century Eastern Mataram period were stolen on 11 September 2013. In March 2020 King Willem-Alexander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> returned the kris of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diponegoro">Prince Diponegoro</a>, leader of the rebellion against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rule that ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java-war">Java War</a> in 1830. A fire on 16 September 2023 collapsed part of the old building and destroyed at least four rooms of precolonial artifacts. The museum reopened on 15 October 2024.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The museum holds about 141,000 objects, from prehistoric artifacts to ceramics, coins, ethnography, history and geography. It acquired them by expeditions, excavations, private collections, gifts and treasures taken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> military campaigns against kingdoms in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombok">Lombok</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>. Its collection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> stone sculpture dates from the Tarumanagara period in the 5th century to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> period in the 15th, and includes a statue of Adityavarman as Bhairava over four metres tall. Its Chinese ceramics run from the Han to the Qing, in the largest ceramic collection in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. The treasure rooms hold gold objects such as the 9th-century Wonoboyo hoard found near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prambanan">Prambanan</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “National Museum of Indonesia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The stone pillar set up for Sunda’s 1522 treaty with Portugal was rediscovered in Jakarta in 1918 and is now kept in the <b>National Museum of Indonesia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sunda-kingdom">Sunda Kingdom · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
